{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1483059-playing-with-combat-mechanics-in-v20","id":1483059,"title":"Playing with combat mechanics in V20.","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Vampire: The Masquerade"],"posts":[{"date":"2022-06-04T12:05:57-07:00","id":1483059,"title":"Playing with combat mechanics in V20.","content":"Some mechanics tweaks I've been thinking about implementing in some One-Offs since the &quot;First strike Problem&quot; thread(not that any of the below solves this). Looking for suggestions.<ol class=\"decimal\"><li><b>Main Change</b> that others revolve around: Altering Disciplines, primarily the three &quot;Physical&quot; disciplines. All three give the standard passive dice-pool increase and Now all allow a vampire to reflexively spend blood to get {Discipline Rank} successes on a roll. Elder powers are handled as per DAV(both 20 and Guides to High/Low clans).</li> <li><i>Combat Change</i>: Combat between vampires defaults to Dice-splitting. All vampires have five turns of combat each round(in order of initiative), increasing difficulty by 1 and removing 1 dice from all pools after turn 1(on the third turn, all rolls are +2 diff and -2 to pools). Every time a round ends, initiative is re-rolled.</li> <li><i>Blood spending:</i> Blood(and willpower) spending limits are <b>Per-Round</b>(not per turn), any discipline power only takes up a maximum of one turn during the round, and discipline use is only limited by Blood spending limits. The boosts from Physicals is also effected, blood spent on Potence and Celerity only provides their bonus per turn, Fortitude lasts the round.</li> <li><i>Dice Tweaks</i>: Physical stats for vampires are no longer rolled for mundane tasks(mostly to lower dice rolling in general). A player making a roll for an attack will only need to roll their ability + relevant discipline dice and, so long as they get at least 1 success, adds their physical ability score as a number of automatic successes. Players without a relevant ability get to roll 1 die at a difficulty determined by the storyteller. For soaking, this means stamina becomes a minimum number of successes an opponent must overcome to do any damage. Dice pool penalties come out of this &quot;Automatic Success&quot; pool first</li> </ol>Any thoughts?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6383-prometheas","name":"Prometheas","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1101"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-06-04T12:18:53-07:00","id":1483060,"title":"","content":"What lead to the &quot;five turns per round&quot; concept? It seem high, and most characters will having exceptionally bad rolls in the later turns without some ability to reduce difficulties. Given the default diff of 6, 3 or 4 turns seems more natural to not inherently push people into diff 10+.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-06-04T12:26:05-07:00","id":1483061,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1483059-playing-with-combat-mechanics-in-v20?p=1483060#post1483060\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What lead to the &quot;five turns per round&quot; concept? It seem high, and most characters will having exceptionally bad rolls in the later turns without some ability to reduce difficulties. Given the default diff of 6, 3 or 4 turns seems more natural to not inherently push people into diff 10+.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Yeah, It was mostly a ballpark number anyway.<br /> <br /> I'm trying to gear the system for encouraging the players to keep things in reserve rather than go all out trying to kill the opponent as quickly as possible. Most of the thought process behind that was to make things just tanky enough that players would need to be using physical disciplines to get around the difficulty of later turns.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6383-prometheas","name":"Prometheas","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1101"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-06-04T12:26:12-07:00","id":1483062,"title":"","content":"Probably not the kind of feedback you were looking for, but many of the problems with combat in the groups I played with were solved by trying to vary the types of combat, rather than the mechanics for it.<br /> <br /> For example, many people have their games combat more or less happen with everyone facing each other in a room or field, the characters may banter back and forth a bit, but everyone just attacks in a big ball of fangs and dice. The NPCs always seem to fight to the last man and never seem to understand the idea of retreating when they are clearly losing the fight at the 50% mark.<br /> <br /> I tend to embrace the &quot;First Strike Problem&quot; as a feature rather than a bug. Thus I encourage the use of snipers, premade ambushes, setting traps, and other methods of fighting without actually being there. This also means the PCs are highly aware of retreating first is your primary option since direct combat is too dangerous most of the time. This also makes Elysium much more valuable since everyone agrees to make it a non-combat area.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/727-thoth","name":"Thoth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar727_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1689"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-06-04T13:43:10-07:00","id":1483066,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Thoth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1483059-playing-with-combat-mechanics-in-v20?p=1483062#post1483062\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Probably not the kind of feedback you were looking for, but many of the problems with combat in the groups I played with were solved by trying to vary the types of combat, rather than the mechanics for it.<br /> <br /> For example, many people have their games combat more or less happen with everyone facing each other in a room or field, the characters may banter back and forth a bit, but everyone just attacks in a big ball of fangs and dice. The NPCs always seem to fight to the last man and never seem to understand the idea of retreating when they are clearly losing the fight at the 50% mark.<br /> <br /> I tend to embrace the &quot;First Strike Problem&quot; as a feature rather than a bug. Thus I encourage the use of snipers, premade ambushes, setting traps, and other methods of fighting without actually being there. This also means the PCs are highly aware of retreating first is your primary option since direct combat is too dangerous most of the time. This also makes Elysium much more valuable since everyone agrees to make it a non-combat area.</div> </div> </div> </div>This is Very solid advice, but I'm also doing this as a mad science experiment to see what happens.<br /> <br /> Half the fun of a game is playing with the mechanics seeing how far they bend before they break..","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6383-prometheas","name":"Prometheas","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1101"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-06-04T22:56:35-07:00","id":1483107,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Prometheas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1483059-playing-with-combat-mechanics-in-v20?p=1483066#post1483066\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Half the fun of a game is playing with the mechanics seeing how far they bend before they break..</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes, yes it is..... I think there are a few forum goers who are still traumatized with the stuff I used to do with 2nd Edition Exalted manses. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/727-thoth","name":"Thoth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar727_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1689"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-06-05T00:51:03-07:00","id":1483111,"title":"","content":"Currently, I do the following (bear in mind I use the Exalted core system of TN7, 10s double, 1s only count for botches, but all modifiers are dice modifiers instead of having both dice modifiers and difficulty as a factor of successes):<br /> <br /> 1. Actions: Everyone gets one major action, one move and one minor action (e.g., opening a door or stowing a weapon). You can switch any of these for an extra minor action or move, but you can't take an extra major action. This allows you to do up to three minor actions without moving, or three moves without doing anything else. Celerity adds one minor action or move per dot as an active effect (which costs vitae).<br /> <br /> 2. Zones: Combat is broken into a number of Zones, representing a section of like terrain, a single enclosed or bounded area, or narrative features (a crumbling balcony, a rain-slicked roof, a yawning chasm, etc). A character can move freely around their current Zone or cross over into an adjacent Zone with a single move. Difficult terrain requires a Dexterity + Athletics roll to move across, which eats up your main action. A critical success (5+ successes) lets you move an additional Zone. Contests (such as a pursuit) are opposed rolls, provided both parties use the same number of moves per turn (otherwise, the one who takes the most moves wins automatically), with a winning pursuer catching the target and engaging them and a winning quarry remaining disengaged and moving an extra Zone away (or two on a critical success).<br /> <br /> 3. Opposition: All attacks are opposed, but defenders take a -1 die penalty for every additional attack they oppose. All-out defence uses all three action types but it gives you a bonus of (Wits) dice to your defence, and no onslaught penalties. You can take half instead of rolling defences if you want things to go faster (my players enjoy rolling the dice when it's not their turn, as it keeps them engaged).<br /> <br /> 4. Escalation: At the start of each round, the winning side (highest number of remaining combatants, most opposed rolls won, etc) gains a pool of bonus dice equal to the number of combatants over their opposition, plus +1-3 as a bonus based on how well they fared. These can be used by anyone on that side as they wish to augment rolls or add +3 initiative per die at the start of the round.<br /> <br /> 5. Damage: Both damage and soak are static. You do your full damage dice pool as damage, and this is reduced by your opponent's full soak. All V20 listed damage is assumed to be what humans take; vamps downgrade the listed damage one more level: bashing is halved, lethal is unhalved bashing, aggravated is lethal. This means teeth and claws, Protean 2 and certain Quietus powers all now do aggravated to humans and lethal to vampires. The exceptions are the three vampiric banes of fire, sunlight and True Faith, which are always aggravated; powers which create fire and sunlight therefore also do aggravated damage.<br /> <br /> 6. Others: Other house rules that impact combat include:<br /> 6a. Where V20 says +1 difficulty, it's -1 die. Where it says -1 difficulty, it's +1 die. Where several mods stack, I tend to just cap modifiers to +/-2 (minor), +/-4 (moderate) and +/-6 dice (major). This keeps everything nice and easy.<br /> 6b. Initiative is rolled once and there's no declaration phase. <br /> 6c. Active Fortitude allows you to add a number of additional Bruised health levels equal to your rating for the scene (damage in these boxes disappears at the end of the scene). You can spend another blood point to reduce wound penalties up to your Fortitude rating for a single round. <br /> 6d. You can heal one lethal or two bashing per blood point. <br /> 6e. As mentioned above, Celebrity's active effect allows you to add additional minor actions or moves (one per dot) at the cost of one blood point per turn. Out of combat, one blood point allows a scene of movement at your normal speed multiplied by (1+Celerity), allowing you travel great distances with ease.<br /> 6f. Discipline blood costs break the per-turn limit, but you can't mix them with other uses of blood expenditure if you went over your limit. <br /> 6g. Blood Surge is +2 dice per blood point per turn. <br /> 6h. Auspex 1 adds passive dice to Perception, like the Physical Disciplines, but doesn't get the auto-successes active effect (it retains the Spidey Sense and Seeing the Unseen benefits) and still has the same drawbacks (which only apply on a botched Perception roll). This does make Auspex very powerful, but as Perception is usually reactive, I think this is okay.<br /> 6h. Aiming requires a move. It adds Perception. With Auspex, this makes shooting a much better idea (but it's still unhalved bashing for vamps, so it's not OP; it has to wraparound before becoming lethal).<br /> 6i. Gun damage isn't halved. It's bashing, as any other lethal damage to mortals would be, and that's enough. But also, fists aren't weirdly as powerful as gunshots now.<br /> 6j. Vamps can see in the dark, and VTR2e's Kindred Senses and Lashing Out.<br /> <br /> And I think that's it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer, publisher, performer<br /> Mostly he/his, sometimes she/her IRL <a href=\"https://adam-lowe.com\" target=\"_blank\">https://adam-lowe.com</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5291-adambeyoncelowe","name":"adambeyoncelowe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5291_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-06-05T02:36:09-07:00","id":1483115,"title":"","content":"Goals for my rules: Make everything better without changing the game (IE breaking anything, IE change conservatively)<br /> My rules in simple:<br /> <br /> <b>Everyone</b>: <br /> 1- Don't roll for damage/Soak, just take half the number. Saves lots of time once you get into the swing of things. Also makes combat more predictable.<br /> 2- Cap the amount of carry-over to 4. Five successes is a phenomenal success, 8 successes is... an equally phenomenal success to 5 successes. <br /> 3- Reduce the difficulty increase of precise Targeting by 1. Wave it completely in some circumstances. <br /> 4- Supernaturals can soak aggravated damage with stamina so long as it isn't their bane (silver, sunlight, faith ETC) It's still horrible to heal. <br /> 5- The storyteller can have everyone lists their size and weight. This may effect difficulties, though supernaturals tend to mitigate the downsides of being too smaller/bigger with powers like Potence. <br /> 6- Armour that is well designed for dealing with a threat (say, a Kevlar vest vs a 9mm) grants double die in protection. <br /> <br /> Humans:<br /> Humans can and will bleed to death. <br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Vampires<br /> <br /> Celerity</b><br /> I see a lot of people suggesting it, but <b>Automatic successes for Celerity is just too strong</b>. <br /> Spending a blood on celerity gives Half-Again the dicepool, and half that the next blood spent; round up, minimum 1 blood.<br /> If you have 5 celerity<br /> No blood: 5 celerity die<br /> 1 blood: 8 celerity die (+3)<br /> 2 blood: 10 celerity die (+2)<br /> 3 blood: 11 celerity die (+1)<br /> 4 blood: 12 celerity die<br /> 5 blood: 13 celerity die. <br /> <br /> 4 celerity: +2, +1, +1,+1<br /> 3 celerity: +2, +1, +1<br /> 2 celerity: +1, +1<br /> 1 celerity: +1<br /> <br /> <b>Fortitude</b>:<br /> Fortitude grants die to resist being knocked down or moved against the vampire's will. <br /> Fortitude dice automatically succeeds against soaking electricity and reduce the resulting spasms: Someone with 4 Fortitude can happily touch railway wires without issue. <br /> When convenient, Fortitude protects clothes at level 2, small items at level 3, larger objects and close cover at 4/5. The vampire will never benefit from their Fortitude twice. This is a masquerade convenience. <br /> <br /> <b>Potence</b>:<br /> If your Potence is greater than your target's armour Class + Fortitude, ignore rule 6. <br /> Your Potence subtracts from any enemy's remain-standing Fortitude dice. <br /> <br /> <b>Obfuscate</b><br /> Obfuscate 1 has been replaced. It now serves to make the vampire less memorable, less important and more difficult to track. Generally, people acknowledge the vampire's presence but assume he's meant to be there and there's not much wrong or noteworthy. Witnesses are more likely to remember things wrongly and investigators are more likely to chase the wrong leads. Obfuscate 1 works perfectly well in a high stress scenarios like violence, which already shake witness testimony. These powers get stronger with Obfuscate level (more dice) It's a wonderful masquerade tool. <br /> Obfuscate 2 works on wilful ignorance, and thus will never allow a vampire to launch an attack. few are going to wilfully ignore an attack. <br /> <br /> <b>Obtenebration</b>: <br /> All obtenebration recedes from fire of a higher soak difficulty than the vampire's Obtenebration level. Faith instantly destroys Obtenebration. Bright light weakens Obtenebration. <br /> Shadowplay grants 1 tendril per level of Obten, for the purpose of multiple targets. <br /> Shroud uses blood to create, It can be beaten by Auspex or Protean. Fast disruptions (gunfire/swinging melee attacks) give people clues on attacker locations (because Fluff wise, Shroud is way too powerful for it's rules otherwise)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary","name":"MyWifeIsScary","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25794_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3711"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-06-05T04:16:20-07:00","id":1483117,"title":"","content":"If it's simplicity we want, I would only change the following:<br /> <br /> 1. Take half for damage and soak. These two rolls slow a lot down. If you use full static ratings instead, as V20DA suggests in their streamlined combat rules, this makes things deadlier, and not everyone likes that (though I do).<br /> 2. Ignore multiple actions. Everyone can do one minor action in addition to their main action, or two minor actions if they give up their action. Maybe three if they also waive their move. You don't need more actions than that, realistically. Similarly, Celerity shouldn't give extra actions. For active effects, let it make you faster (multiply speed by [1+Celerity]), jump the initiative queue, and give a number of free dodges or minor actions per turn* as your Celerity rating (without penalty).<br /> 3. Roll initiative once. Ignore the declaration phase.<br /> 4. Vamps soak all aggravated with Stamina + Fortitude, except their banes. This makes claws and teeth deadly, but not necessarily OP.<br /> <br /> That's it. That's all you need to make V20 combat work. My comment upthread was what I personally do, and adds a lot of extra things in, but it isn't for everyone. I think the above rules make things a lot easier and faster, and should please most tables.<br /> <br /> *Okay, I was trying to keep it simple, but I can't resist adding a bit of crunch back in. If you have opposed combat rolls, you can allow active Celerity to turn a number of successful parries/blocks as your Celerity dots into *attacks* using your margin of success. So if you get attacked five times, parry three times, and lose twice, you could turn those three successful defences into an attack with your margin of success as your successes on the to-hit roll (doing damage according to the weapon you defended with, or your fists/claws if unarmed). It's almost as good as an extra attack, but not quite, it keys off a successful defence roll first; and because you're using opposed rolls anyway, you don't need to roll anything extra to resolve this. Just add the damage up as usual.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer, publisher, performer<br /> Mostly he/his, sometimes she/her IRL <a href=\"https://adam-lowe.com\" target=\"_blank\">https://adam-lowe.com</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5291-adambeyoncelowe","name":"adambeyoncelowe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5291_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2802"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}